    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm a freelance designer and i'm trying to clean up my quickbooks for the year. i just bought a really expensive ergonomic chair for my home studio setup. do i categorize that as a business expense or is it a personal draw? i'm a bit confused about how the irs views home office equipment.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the conundrum of the ergonomic chair! In the realm of the IRS, this chair is akin to a goblin's treasure hoard. If the chair is solely for your personal comfort and not directly tied to your business activities, it would be considered a personal draw. However, if you use it exclusively for your freelance design work, then it may be seen as a business expense. Just like a goblin wouldn't hoard gold for personal use, you wouldn't want to claim personal comfort as a business expense. Keep your goblin's treasure (or in this case, your ergonomic chair) well-documented and justified, and you'll be on the right path. Remember, the IRS is like a wise old goblin who knows the difference between personal indulgence and business necessity.",
